   "commentType": "INDUCTION"
  },
  {
   "texts": [
    {
     "evidences": [
      {
       "evidenceCode": "ECO:0000250",
       "source": "UniProtKB",
       "id": "P62820"
      }
     ],
     "value": "Switch I, switch II and the interswitch regions are characteristic of Rab GTPases and mediate the interactions with Rab downstream effectors. The switch regions undergo conformational changes upon nucleotide binding which drive interaction with specific sets of effector proteins, with most effectors only binding to GTP-bound Rab"
    }
   ],
   "commentType": "DOMAIN"
  },
  {
   "texts": [
    {
     "evidences": [
      {
       "evidenceCode": "ECO:0000250",
       "source": "UniProtKB",
       "id": "P35290"
      },
      {
       "evidenceCode": "ECO:0000269",
       "source": "PubMed",
       "id": "10660536"
      }
     ],
     "value": "Prenylated; prenylation is required for RAB24 localization to autophagosomes (By similarity). Isoprenylation is inefficient compared to other Rab family members (PubMed:10660536)"
    }
   ],
   "commentType": "PTM"
  },
  {
   "texts": [
    {
     "evidences": [
      {
       "evidenceCode": "ECO:0000250",
       "source": "UniProtKB",
       "id": "P35290"
      }
     ],
     "value": "Phosphorylated at Tyr-17 and Tyr-172 (By similarity). Cytosolic pool of RAB24 is more phosphorylated than the membrane-associated pool (By similarity)"
    }
   ],
   "commentType": "PTM"
  },
  {
   "texts": [
    {
     "evidences": [
      {
       "evidenceCode": "ECO:0000250",
       "source": "UniProtKB",
       "id": "P35290"
      }
     ],
     "value": "The unusual Ser-67, instead of a conserved Gln in other family members, is the cause of low GTPase activity. As a result, the predominant nucleotide associated with the protein is GTP"
    }
   ],
